\newpage
\begin{verbatim}

% RULE RComplSentSubst
< m1: VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec1}
               [mu1,
                I2:predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                          [I1:mu2,
                           complrel/SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1},
                           mu3 
                          ],
                mu5
               ]
  
  SUBST:
  m2: SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[mu4]
>

< m : VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec1}
               [mu1,
                I3:predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                          [I1:mu2,
                           complrel/SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[mu4],
                           mu3 
                          ],
                mu5
               ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: NOT EXIST (mu2, [ I11:head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu6] ])
         I11: VERBrec1.subc = modalverb
       I2: SENTENCEVARrec1.index = LEVEL AND
           ( SENTENCEVARrec1 <> { finiteness : finite
                                  senttype   : subordinateclause
                                  mood       : declarative
                                  conjkey    : 0 }
             OR
             EXIST (mu2, [I31:head/VERB{VERBrec2}[mu7]])
           )
       I3: SENTENCErec1 <> { finiteness : finite
                             senttype   : subordinateclause
                             mood       : declarative
                             conjkey    : 0 }
           OR
           EXIST (mu2, [I31:head/VERB{VERBrec2}[mu7]]) 
       m2: SENTENCErec1 <> { finiteness : finite
                             senttype   : subordinateclause
                             mood       : declarative
                             conjkey    : 0 }
           OR
           ( NOT EXIST (mu4, [subjrel/..]) AND
             EXIST (mu4, [shiftrel/..]) )
         I31: VERBrec2.thatdel = yesdel
      >

   PARAMETERS
      <
       LEVEL
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCEVARrec1 = COPYT_sentenceTOvar(SENTENCErec1) 
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: SENTENCEVARrec1 := COPYT_sentenceTOvar(SENTENCErec1);
              SENTENCEVARrec1.index := LEVEL
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RComplSentSubst
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}\\
\begin{enumerate}
\item CLOSEDINFSENT: (always: voice = active) \\
(vp120): x1 see x2 $\rightarrow$ x1 saw [the man leave]
\item OPENINFSENT:\\
?? (all examples concern modals, which are covered in a separate rule)
\item CLOSEDTOSENT:\\
(vp010): appear x1 $\rightarrow$ appear [he to be your friend]\\
(vp120): x1 expect x2 $\rightarrow$ x1 expect [you to come]
\item OPENTOSENT:\\
(vp120): x1 decide x2 $\rightarrow$ x1 decided [x1 to leave]\\
(vp123): x1 promise/persuade x2 x3 $\rightarrow$ x1 promise/persuade x2 [x1/x2 
to see a doctor] (x2 is indobj)\\
(vp123): x1 force x2 x3 $\rightarrow$ x1 force x2 [x2 to cooperate] (x2 is obj)
\item FORTOSENT:\\
(vp120): x1 prefer x2 $\rightarrow$ x1 prefers [for John to go]
\item THATSENT:\\
(vp010): seem x1 $\rightarrow$ seem [that John comes]\\
(vp120): x1 know x2 $\rightarrow$ x1 know [that he will come]\\
(vp012): seem x1 to x2 $\rightarrow$ seem [that he is ill] to x2\\
(vp123): x1 tell x2 x3 $\rightarrow$ x1 tell x2 [that he would be back early]\\
Note: an extra test on the presence or absence of the conj THAT will be performed in 
the extraposition rules, since only sentences with the conjunction `that' may 
extrapose. \\
In the present system, only sentences with THAT are generated, but in 
analysis a sentence without THAT may occur, provided the verb does allow it (
has `yesdel' as value for the attribute `thatdel')
\item QSENT:\\
(vp120): x1 wonder x2 $\rightarrow$ x1 wonder [where to go]\\
(vp123): x1 ask x2 x3 $\rightarrow$ x1 ask x2 [if he wanted tea]
\item CLOSEDGERUND:\\
(vp120): x1 watch x2 $\rightarrow$ x1 watch [the moon rising over the 
mountains]
\item OPENGERUND:\\
(vp120): x1 burst out x2 $\rightarrow$ x1 burst out [x1 singing]
\item ASIFSENT:\\
(vp010): appear x1 $\rightarrow$ appear [as if he will win]\\
(vp120): x1 act x2 $\rightarrow$ x1 act [as if he had gone mad]
\item ITTHATSENT:\\
(vp120): x1 believe it x2 $\rightarrow$ x1 believe it [that I've won the 
lottery ]
\end{enumerate}
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) english
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] VerbppropToClause
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_
PropSubst
\item[* date of creation:] 01-FEB-1988 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] ROpenNPPropSubst
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 12/09/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted conditions on absence of `that' in generation: \\
1) (A) if main verb (in mu2) allows it (= yesdel)\\
2) (G) if main verb (in mu2) allows it and there is a shifted 
   subject in the embedded clause: Whom did you say left?\\
Problem: `acknowledge' etc = nodel; can thus not have questioning of embedded 
subject (*Whom did they acknowledge (that) was defeated)
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
