\newpage
\begin{verbatim}

% RULE RLdislocSubjSentSubst
< m1: N1 [subjrel/I2::SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1},
          mu2
         ]
  
  SUBST:
  m2: I1::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[mu3]
>

< m : N1 [leftdislocrel/I1::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[mu3],
          subjrel/NP{NPrec1}
                 [head/DEMPRO(KEY_thatDEMPROkey){DEMPROrec1}],
          mu2
         ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: SENTENCErec1.adverbial = false AND
           ( ( SENTENCErec1.PROsubject = true AND
               SENTENCErec1.infsort = toinf ) OR
             SENTENCErec1.finiteness = finite OR 
             SENTENCErec1.infsort = fortoinf ) AND
           SENTENCErec1 <> { finiteness : finite
                             mood       : declarative
                             senttype   : subordinateclause
                             conjkey    : 0     }
           AND
           SENTENCErec1 <> { mood       : whinterrogative
                             senttype   : subordinateclause
                             conjkey    : KEY_ifconjkey   }

       I2: SENTENCEVARrec1.adverbial = false AND
           SENTENCEVARrec1.index = LEVEL AND
           ( ( SENTENCEVARrec1.PROsubject = true AND
               SENTENCEVARrec1.infsort = toinf ) OR
             SENTENCEVARrec1.finiteness = finite OR
             SENTENCEVARrec1.infsort = fortoinf ) AND
           SENTENCEVARrec1 <> { finiteness : finite
                                mood       : declarative
                                senttype   : subordinateclause
                                conjkey    : 0     }
           AND
           SENTENCEVARrec1 <> { mood       : whinterrogative
                                senttype   : subordinateclause
                                conjkey    : KEY_ifconjkey   }
       m1: N1.CAT IN [ VERBPPROP, NPPROP, ADJPPROP, PREPPPROP,
                       ADVPPROP] AND
           ( N1.CAT IN [VERBPPROP] -> 
                  SENTENCEVARrec1.infsort <> fortoinf )
       m : N1.CAT IN [ VERBPPROP, NPPROP, ADJPPROP, PREPPPROP,
                       ADVPPROP] AND
           ( N1.CAT IN [VERBPPROP] -> 
                  SENTENCErec1.infsort <> fortoinf )
      >

   PARAMETERS
      <
       LEVEL
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCEVARrec1 = COPYT_sentenceTOvar(SENTENCErec1) 
          A1: NPrec1 := AUX_NPONthatDMPR
              (* number is specified as singular *)
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: NPrec1 = AUX_NPONthatDMPR
              (* including number = singular *)
          A1: SENTENCEVARrec1 := COPYT_sentenceTOvar(SENTENCErec1);
              SENTENCEVARrec1.index := LEVEL
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RLdislocSubjSentSubst
\item[* relevant examples:] (see Quirk, pp 735-40)\\
\begin{enumerate}
\item THATSENT:\\
That he went away so early THAT surprised me.\\
Note that in generation, no test is needed, since the SENTENCEVAR has been 
specified as having a THATconjkey. In analysis, however, both forms may try to 
occur
\item QSENT{whinterrogative}:\\
How the book will sell THAT depends on its author
\item QSENT{yesnointerrogative}:\\
Whether it rains or not THAT does not concern me\\
Note that in this case, the wh-sent may not start with `if', although otherwise 
`if' and `whether' are considered interchangeable
\item TOINF:\\
To be a member of this club THAT is a privilege. 
\item FORTOINF (not for VERBPPROPs):\\
For a bridge to collapse like that THAT (is) unbelievable / a shame
\end{enumerate}
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) english
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] VerbppropToClause
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_
PropSubst
\item[* date of creation:] 01-FEB-1988 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] RComplSentSubst
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 23/03/88\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders. Dummy `that'.
\item date: 19/02/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders. Restricted the set of 
possible open sentences to {\em toinf\/}s. Even fortoinfs are excluded, since 
they seem to occur only as subject of an ADJPPROP: For a bridge to collapse 
like that is unbelievable.
\item date: 21/03/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders. Generalized the rule to 
deal with all XPPROPs as input (note that xpprops are changed into CLAUSEs only 
in the next rule class). For ADJPPROPs and NPPROPs, a ForToInf is also possible
; hence, this infsort has been added again to the possible input infsorts (cf.\ 
what was said in the previous modification)
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] put a subject SENTENCE in a leftdislocrel. This sentence can not 
remain in subject position, since it does not behave like one: it cannot form a 
question without extraposition ($^{*}${\em Did that he went away surprise you?
\/}), and it cannot form the subject of a subordinate clause ($^{*}${\em I did 
not realise that that he had gone away would upset you\/}). However, for the 
subject-verb agreement rules it is easiest to have a subject in all 
structures; therefore, the dummy `that' is inserted. It will be removed later (
somewhere after SubjVerbAgr).
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) Only input 
models without any ADVVAR (or anything else) as lefthand sister of the subject 
are accepted.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of 
parameters) The parameter LEVEL is used to indicate the depth of embedding
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] 
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
It is assumed that openIngs cannot occur as leftdislocrel sentences. Either 
they are an NP and hence remain in subject position (Eating people is wrong; Is 
eating people wrong?) or they are sentences but must obligatorily be extraposed 
(It wasn't easy getting the equipment loaded; it is wrong eating people). 
However, it seems that openIngs only occur as the subject of ADJPPROPs, so they 
do not occur here anyway.
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\

\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

