% RULE RExtrapSubjSentSubst
< m1: VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec1}
               [I8:mu8,
                subjrel/I2::SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1},
                I9:mu1,
                predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                       [mu2,
                        head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu3],
                        I1:mu4,
                        I3:mu7
                       ],
                mu6
               ]
  
  SUBST:
  m2: I4::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[mu5]
>

< m : VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec1}
               [I8:mu8,
                I9:mu1,
                predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                       [mu2,
                        head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu3],
                        I1:mu4,
                        complrel/I4::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[mu5],
                        I3:mu7
                       ],
                mu6
               ]
>


   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: NOT EXIST (mu4, [ I11:rel1/..])
           I11: rel1 = complrel OR
                rel1 IN AUX_finalVPAdvRels
       I2: SENTENCEVARrec1.index = LEVEL AND
           SENTENCEVARrec1.adverbial = false AND
           ( ( SENTENCEVARrec1.PROsubject = true AND
               SENTENCEVARrec1.infsort = toinf 
             ) OR
             SENTENCEVARrec1.finiteness = finite )
       I3: ALL (mu7, I31:rel2/..)
           I31: rel2 IN AUX_finalVPAdvRels
       I4: SENTENCErec1.adverbial = false AND
           ( ( SENTENCErec1.PROsubject = true AND
               SENTENCErec1.infsort = toinf 
             ) OR
             SENTENCErec1.finiteness = finite )
       I8: ALL (mu8, leftdislocrel/..)
       I9: NOT EXIST (mu1, [leftdislocrel/..])
      >

   PARAMETERS
      <
       LEVEL
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCEVARrec1 = COPYT_sentenceTOvar(SENTENCErec1)
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: SENTENCEVARrec1 := COPYT_sentenceTOvar(SENTENCErec1);
              SENTENCEVARrec1.index := LEVEL
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RExtrapSubjSentSubst
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}\\
\begin{enumerate}
\item THATSENT:\\
x1 surprise x2 $\rightarrow$ (it) surprises me (that) he never told you
\item OPENTOSENT:\\
x1 surprise x2 $\rightarrow$ (it) surprised me to see you there
\item QSENT(wh):\\
x1 depend x2 $\rightarrow$ (it) depends on the author how the book will sell
\item QSENT(yesno):\\
x1 concern x2 $\rightarrow$ (it) does not concern me if/whether it rains
\end{enumerate}
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) english
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] VerbppropToClause
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_
PropSubst
\item[* date of creation:] 01-FEB-1988 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] RComplSentSubst
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 19/02/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders. Imposed conditions on 
the kind of sentence allowed (same as in Ldislocrel, though less strict: the 
conjunction `that' may be missing, and the conjunction `if' may be used in 
stead of `whether'). 
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] put a subject SENTENCE in a complrel in the VP, so that it can 
be extraposed in the SentOKRules. This rule is an 
alternative to the previous rule, that put a subject SENTENCE in leftdislocrel.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) Note that 
openIngs and FortoSents are not allowed here. In the subject substitution of 
ADJPPROPs they do occur: {\em It was not easy getting the equipment loaded; It 
was considered impossible for anyone to escape\/}.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of 
parameters) The parameter LEVEL is used to indicate the depth of embedding
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] 
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
It is assumed that the moved sentence should indeed be placed after all 
other elements in the VERBP, but not outside the VERBP. When the ADVVARrules 
etc have been written, this assumption should be checked. Note, however, that 
the extraposition rules will move the SENT wherever it is inside the VP.
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item remarks:\\
Quirk notices that `that' may be used instead of `it', if the initial sentence 
is closed by a comma: {\em That was a mistake, to let him go\/}(p.633). This 
kind of rightdislocation will not be dealt with for the moment.
\item problems:\\

\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

