\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RModalComplSentSubst1
\item[* relevant examples:] one place modals: can (x1 see x2) $\rightarrow$ x1 
can see x2
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) english
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class): TC\_SentOKrules
\item[* date of creation:] 25-FEB-1988 13:54:05  
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:]
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 15/2/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted the model to allow for a negrel in the embedded sentence. Needed for 
`narrow scope' modals like {\em may(010)\/}. See below under `problems'.
\item date: 30/10/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Added `extraposrel' as possible final element of embedded sentence (i.e.\ after
mu9). Needed for a modal such as {\em will\/} + {\em It be possible that he 
swims\/} (hij zal kunnen zwemmen). The surface order of extraposrel, 
postsentadvrel and a tempadvrel heading a sentence may still be wrong, though!
\item date: 05/01/90\ \ \ \ \ author: Lisette Appelo\\
Deleted condition on absence of retro tempvar.
\item date: 07/05/91\ \ \ \ \ author: Petra de Wit\\
adapted rule to copy adjunct values
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to insert an embedded sentence for modals, and at the same time 
prune the higher VERBP, so that the modal becomes an auxiliary. Pruning is 
needed because in surface structure, no difference exists between sentences 
like {\em Did you see him\/} and {\em Can you see him\/}. In deep structure, a 
difference is assumed for reasons of isomorphism, since some modal 
constructions may have to translate into real subsentences: {\em I can swim\/} 
has as one of its meanings {\em Het is mogelijk dat ik zwem\/}.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:] To prune the higher verbp only after the 
time rules etc.\ in the SentOKrules. Reason: Lisette expects to be able to copy 
Dutch {\em graag/like\/} rules for this case.
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] In generation, the exact 
value of VERBPPROPrec1.synvpefs is unknown (although it must be one element of 
a very restricted subset, viz.\ the intersection of the verbpatterns of the 
modal (probably only one or two anyway) and the set of closed infinitive 
complements defined in the auxdomain). In analysis, the value is set to the 
whole of this intersection.
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
It is assumed that in the case of modals the surface parser will assign to the 
main SENTENCE (and thus to the VERBPPROPrec2 in this model) the 
synpatterns and thetapattern of the head verb, not of the modal. However, the 
aktionsart of the modal should be passed on to the SENTENCE. Note that it is 
also assumed that the surface parser will assign the passive voice to a 
SENTENCE with a modal (itself active) and an embedded passive.
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
The occurrence of adv(vars) in the two sentences still has to be taken care of. 
For the moment, it is assumed that in the VERBP of a modal, no beginVPAdvRels 
occur, nor any finalVPAdvRels (nor anything else except the complrel/SENT).
Also, 
it is assumed that the VERBPPROP of the modal has only a very restricted set 
of postVPADVVARs (for cases like {\em He may 
[have worked yesterday]\/}, where {\em yesterday\/} can only belong to the 
embedded sentence, since {\em may\/} is a present tense and cannot take a past 
adverbial. These adverbials all should be referential.) It is 
assumed that in the embedded SENTENCE only ADVPs occur, while the higher 
sentence still has only ADVPVARs etc.\\
The rule has been adapted to allow for negrels in the embedded sentence. This 
is needed for a few modals like `may', which have a `narrow scope' reading:
{\em he may not come\/} has as one of its meanings {\em it is possible that he 
does not come\/}. However, other 010 modals like {\em can\/} lack this option:
{\em He can not come\/} does not mean {\em It is possible that he does not come
\/}. No provisions have been made to deal with this problem, which would 
probably require either a new attribute(value) for modals, or an enumeration in 
CA-pairs of the modals which may and which may not occur with a negrel in the 
embedded sentence. For detailed info and extra problems, see Quirk 7.52.
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

