\begin{verbatim}

% RULE RConjPrepNPSubst
< m1:     I3::N1
               [ leftdislocrel/I1::PREPPVAR{PREPPVARrec1},
                 mu2,
                 I4:rel1/N2[mu3],
                 mu4
               ] 
  SUBST:
  m2: PREPP{PREPPrec1}[I5:mu5]
>

< m :     I3::N1
               [ leftdislocrel/PREPP{PREPPrec1}[I5:mu5,
                                                I2:sig1],
                 mu2,
                 I4:rel1/N2[mu3],
                 mu4
               ] 
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       m : NOT EXIST(mu2, [I11:../T1]) AND
           NOT EXIST(mu3, [I11:../T1]) AND
           NOT EXIST(mu4, [I11:../T1])
         I11: T1.CAT IN AUX_propvarcats OR
              ( T1.CAT IN [NPVAR] WITH 
                T1.REC.NPhead IN [sentNP, openIngNP] )
       I1: PREPPVARrec1.index = LEVEL
       I2: ALL (sig1, puncrel/PUNC(KEY_commaPUNCkey){PUNCrec2})
       I3: N1.CAT IN [VERBPPROP, ADJPPROP, NPPROP, PREPPPROP, ADVPPROP]
       I4: ( rel1 = predrel AND 
             N2.CAT IN [VERBP, ADJP, NP, PREPP, ADVP] ) 
           OR 
           ( rel1 = idrel AND N2.CAT IN [NPVAR, SENTENCEVAR] ) OR
           ( rel1 = objrel AND N2.CAT IN [NPVAR] )
       I5: EXIST (mu5, [objrel/I51::NP{NPrec1}[mu6]])
         I51: NPrec1.NPhead IN [sentNP, openIngNP]
      >

   PARAMETERS
      <
       LEVEL
      >

<
   SUBRULE (* 1. no comma *)
      < >
      < sig1: [] >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: PREPPrec1.actsubcefs * [temp] = []
          A1: PREPPVARrec1 := COPYT_prepptovar(PREPPrec1);
              PREPPVARrec1.index := LEVEL
         >

   SUBRULE (* 2. comma *)
      < >
      < sig1: [puncrel/PUNC(KEY_commaPUNCkey){PUNCrec1}] >

      COMP
         <
          C1: PREPPVARrec1 = COPYT_prepptovar(PREPPrec1) AND
              PREPPrec1.actsubcefs * [temp] = []
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: PREPPrec1.actsubcefs * [temp] = []
          A1: PREPPVARrec1 := COPYT_prepptovar(PREPPrec1);
              PREPPVARrec1.index := LEVEL
         >
>
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RconjPrepNPsubst
\item[* relevant examples:] without my noticing it,...  ; without noticing it, 
he ...
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] xppropTOclause
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_PropSubst
\item[* date of creation:] 2-NOV-1988 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] RConjSentSubst
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 16/11/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted to let all possible heads pass 
\item date: 27/11/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted to stop temporal PREPPs (see RPrepIngSubsent in the ConjSent formation 
rules in the CLAUSEtoSENTENCE subgrammar.)
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to substitute a `subordinate' prepp heading a sentential NP 
for its variable.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) It is assumed 
for the moment that the prepp always remains in leftdislocrel, and that a 
separating comma is obligatory. This comma is optional in 
analysis. The condition on propcats is added to act as a kind of 
substordercondition, to prevent the creation of unnecessary parallel paths in 
analysis. 

For openIng NPs, a special control rule has been written, assuming (for the 
moment) obligatory subject control
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:
Because the position of the conjsent (initial/final) may influence the 
interpretation of anaphoric (esp.\ pronominal) expressions, the mapping of the 
conjsent substitution rules tries to keep the surface order intact: initial 
conjsents are mapped onto initial ones, and final conjsents on final ones. 
Dutch also has a middle conjsent ({\em Hij ging, hoewel hij geen zin had, toch 
maar naar het feest\/}); it is mapped onto an English initial one. However, 
because there may already be something in leftdislocrel, and because there may 
be only one leftdislocrel per sentence, it is mapped also on a final one, but 
with a lowering of the bonus. (Remember that the rules that introduced a 
variable for a conjsent were all mapped onto each other, so that up till here 
there always were two parallel paths for a conjsent, both initial and final.)
\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\

\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

