\newpage
\begin{verbatim}

% RULE RXppExtrapSubjSentSubst
< m1: I1::N1 [I8:mu8,
              subjrel/I2::SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1},
              I9:mu1
             ]
  
  SUBST:
  m2: I4::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[mu5]
>

< m :  I1::N1 [I8:mu8,
               I9:mu1,
               extraposrel/I4::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[mu5]
              ]
>


   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: N1.CAT IN [ADJPPROP, PREPPPROP, NPPROP, ADVPPROP]
       I2: SENTENCEVARrec1.index = LEVEL AND
           SENTENCEVARrec1.adverbial = false AND
           ( ( SENTENCEVARrec1.PROsubject = true AND
               ( SENTENCEVARrec1.infsort = toinf OR
                 SENTENCErec1.infsort = OpenIng )
             ) OR
             SENTENCEVARrec1.finiteness = finite OR
             SENTENCEVARrec1.infsort = fortoinf )
       I4: SENTENCErec1.adverbial = false AND
           ( ( SENTENCErec1.PROsubject = true AND
               ( SENTENCErec1.infsort = toinf OR
                 SENTENCErec1.infsort = OpenIng )
             ) OR
             SENTENCErec1.finiteness = finite OR
             SENTENCErec1.infsort = fortoinf )
       I8: ALL (mu8, leftdislocrel/..)
       I9: NOT EXIST (mu1, [leftdislocrel/..])
      >

   PARAMETERS
      <
       LEVEL
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCEVARrec1 = COPYT_sentenceTOvar(SENTENCErec1)
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: SENTENCEVARrec1 := COPYT_sentenceTOvar(SENTENCErec1);
              SENTENCEVARrec1.index := LEVEL
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RExtrapSubjSentSubst
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}\\
\begin{enumerate}
\item THATSENT:\\
x1 impossible $\rightarrow$ (it is) impossible (that) he never told you
\item OPENTOSENT:\\
x1 impossible $\rightarrow$ (it is) impossible to lift this thing
\item QSENT(wh):\\
x1 unclear $\rightarrow$ (it is) unclear what they were doing there
\item QSENT(yesno):\\
x1 doubtful $\rightarrow$ (it is) doubtful whether she will come
\item FORTOINF:\\
x1 impossible $\rightarrow$ (it is) impossible for a man not to love himself
\item OPENING:\\
x1 easy $\rightarrow$ (it isn't) easy getting this equipment loaded
\end{enumerate}
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) english
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XppropToClause
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_
PropSubst
\item[* date of creation:] 21-MAR-19889
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] RExtrapSubjSentSubst
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] extrapose a subject SENTENCE from an XPPROP (X <> VERB).
This rule is an alternative to the leftdislocrel rule. 
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) In this case, 
the subject sentence is extraposed right away (it is not put in complrel inside 
the XP first, since there seems no need for that). A separate NoExtraposition 
rule is needed to take care that in analysis, the extraposed sentence remains 
extraposed till it gets to the current rule.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of 
parameters) The parameter LEVEL is used to indicate the depth of embedding
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] 
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item remarks:\\
Quirk notices that `that' may be used instead of `it', if the initial sentence 
is closed by a comma: {\em That was foolish, to let him go\/}(p.633). This 
kind of rightdislocation will not be dealt with for the moment.
\item problems:\\

\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

